# npm integrity records: what the packages claim about their own bytes, 2026-10-05

Every package this project publishes for npm now carries a machine-readable statement of
what its own bytes hash to, `bin/integrity.json`, and three readers hold the package to it:
the assembler that writes it, `bin/postinstall.js`, and `bin/chaos` (via
`bin/chaos-bootstrap.js`). `scripts/ci/check-npm-integrity.py` is the fourth reader, the one
that runs before anything is uploaded. This log is the transcript of those four disagreeing
with a tampered tree.

Sections 2 to 10 come from one driver: it builds a six-package tree at the depth the
assembler expects, installs it into a node_modules layout, and then breaks it. The same
sequence, with more cases, is what `scripts/ci/test-assemble-integrity.sh` runs on every CI
run and in the container, so nothing below is only reproducible from this session.

== 1. the four npm suites, on the host

The four suites this batch ships or extends. `test-postinstall.js` drives the shipped
install library in-process, one assertion per way bytes can fail to be the bytes.
`test-check-npm-integrity.py` builds one real six-package tree with the shipped assembler
and breaks exactly one thing per case, so a refusal can only come from the thing the case
changed. `test-assemble-integrity.sh` is the end-to-end one: assembler, guard, installer,
launcher, repair. `test-publish-npm.sh` covers the publish script's refusal to upload what
it cannot check.

    $ node crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/npm/chaos/scripts/test-postinstall.js | tail -1
        49 passed, 0 failed

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-npm-integrity.py | tail -1
        test-check-npm-integrity: OK (45 cases against the guard)

    $ bash scripts/ci/test-assemble-integrity.sh | tail -1
        assemble-integrity guards: OK (assembler, records, the guard's eight refusals, install, launch, repair)

    $ bash scripts/ci/test-publish-npm.sh | tail -1
        publish-npm guards: OK (empty, partial and complete platform sets, and the digest refusals)

== 2. the assembler, on six synthetic release binaries

Six synthetic release binaries -- shell scripts with distinct bytes, so no digest can be
accidentally shared between two targets -- through the shipped assembler. The record shown
is linux-x64's. Both digests reproduce with `sha256sum` against the files on disk, which is
the claim every section after this one leans on. `release_artifact` is the name
`release.yml` uploads the build under; `test-assemble-integrity.sh` compares that field
against the workflow's own matrix, and rule 6 of the guard uses it to look the bytes up in
`SHA256SUMS`.

