== 7. the installed binary truncated to eight bytes

The failure this design exists for: a download or an update that stopped halfway. The
launcher's pre-launch check is a `stat` on size, not a hash -- 150 MB on every invocation is
not a price worth paying for a check an attacker with write access to the home could undo by
editing the record too -- and the size alone is enough to notice. The message names the
length it expected and which package said so, then re-installs from the package before
running.

    $ CHAOS_HOME=/tmp/npm-integrity-evidence-v74ge5e9/home node node_modules/chaos-code/bin/chaos after-truncate
        chaos-code: /tmp/npm-integrity-evidence-v74ge5e9/home/bin/chaos is 8 bytes, not the 53 chaos-code-linux-x64 records; re-installing from the package
        chaos-fixture linux-x64 after-truncate

    $ cmp /tmp/npm-integrity-evidence-v74ge5e9/src/linux-x64 /tmp/npm-integrity-evidence-v74ge5e9/home/bin/chaos-9.9.9 && echo identical
        identical

== 8. one archive byte flipped in the package, with an install already in place

Two different positions, and the design separates them. A package whose archive no longer
matches must not produce a new install (first command, and the `find | wc -l` is there
because `ls` on an empty directory is silent whether or not anything was written), and a
fresh launch out of that package must refuse (last command). An install that was already
verified does still run: its bytes were checked when they were written, and the package has
no authority to invalidate them by being broken afterwards.

    $ CHAOS_HOME=/tmp/npm-integrity-evidence-v74ge5e9/home2 npm_config_user_agent=npm/10.9.0 node node_modules/chaos-code/bin/postinstall.js
