== 9. the package's own record deleted

The record is the root of trust, and its absence is a position rather than a pass. A fresh
install refuses. A reinstall over a home that already holds the binary refuses too -- the
file in place is only checkable through the record, so an installer that could not read it
has no evidence to offer and says so instead of reporting success. The launcher still runs
that installed binary: refusing there would mean a packaging accident stops a shell that
works.

    $ CHAOS_HOME=/tmp/npm-integrity-evidence-v74ge5e9/home3 npm_config_user_agent=npm/10.9.0 node node_modules/chaos-code/bin/postinstall.js
        chaos-code: refusing to install chaos-code-linux-x64: /tmp/npm-integrity-evidence-v74ge5e9/node_modules/chaos-code-linux-x64/bin/integrity.json does not exist
          The package does not say which bytes it is meant to contain, so there is
          nothing to check them against. Re-install from the registry; a package
          assembled without bin/integrity.json must not be published.
        exit=1

    $ CHAOS_HOME=/tmp/npm-integrity-evidence-v74ge5e9/home npm_config_user_agent=npm/10.9.0 node node_modules/chaos-code/bin/postinstall.js
        chaos-code: refusing to install chaos-code-linux-x64: /tmp/npm-integrity-evidence-v74ge5e9/node_modules/chaos-code-linux-x64/bin/integrity.json does not exist
          The package does not say which bytes it is meant to contain, so there is
          nothing to check them against. Re-install from the registry; a package
          assembled without bin/integrity.json must not be published.
        exit=1

    $ CHAOS_HOME=/tmp/npm-integrity-evidence-v74ge5e9/home node node_modules/chaos-code/bin/chaos record-gone-but-installed
