== 11. mutation: 79 single-occurrence patches to the shipped files

Each mutant is one textual patch applied to one shipped file, with the four suites re-run
afterwards, the file restored and compared byte-for-byte against its backup, and the battery
aborting if any restore differs or if the baseline was not green first. The 79 are spread
over six files: `check-npm-integrity.py` (34), `install-lib.js` (24, a file this batch
creates), `assemble-platform-packages.js` (9), `publish-npm.sh` (5), `chaos-bootstrap.js`
(4), `postinstall.js` (3). A surviving mutant is a claim in the code that no test would
notice being wrong, so survivors are listed rather than rounded away.

    Baseline, before any mutant:
    guard: exit=0 :: ['test-check-npm-integrity: OK (45 cases against the guard)']
    assemble: exit=0 :: ["assemble-integrity guards: OK (assembler, records, the guard's eight refusals, install, launch, repair)"]
    unit: exit=0 :: ['49 passed, 0 failed']
    publish: exit=0 :: ['publish-npm guards: OK (empty, partial and complete platform sets, and the digest refusals)']

    Full battery:
    == 78 killed, 1 survived, 79 mutants, every restore byte-identical ==

    The one survivor:
    L12  SURVIVED  unit=GREEN  [install-lib.js] a canonical name that resolves to nothing is reported as installed

L12 removes the post-condition that reports `dangling` when the canonical name does not
resolve after the swap. No caller can reach it: `swapCanonical` either leaves a link or a
copy that resolves, or throws and is reported as `swap`, and the versioned file it points at
exists on every path that reaches the swap. The check stays -- it is the guard against a
filesystem that reports success for a write it did not make, which is what network and FUSE
mounts do -- and the docstring on `installVersionedBinary` now says so explicitly. It is
recorded here as untested, not passed: pinning it would mean faking the filesystem, and a
test against a fake filesystem proves nothing about the real one.

== 12. the same gate inside the container

`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` runs the container copy of the gate, which is the four suites
plus `node --check` on the five shipped JS entry points. The tree line names the last
commit, but the listing behind the checksum is `git ls-files -co --exclude-standard`, so it
covers this working tree -- the five new files included -- rather than `07d51c8c`'s committed
tree. The run first drafted for this section counted 4067 files and ran before this log
existed; the one quoted below is a later run over the tree as it is committed, and this file
is the difference between the two counts.

