== 13. what none of the above proves

Integrity here is tamper-evident, not tamper-proof. The record and the code that verifies it
ship inside the same tarball, so anyone who can rewrite the package can rewrite both; the
digests are signed nowhere. What the checks buy is the accident class -- a truncated upload,
a corrupted cache, a half-written install, an artifact that is not the one the release
hashed -- failing loudly at the first reader instead of producing a binary that runs wrong.

The trust anchor for a release lives outside npm: `release-bins/SHA256SUMS`, and the ed25519
`.sig` sidecar signed for each artifact and verified by
`scripts/verify-release-signature.sh`. The `release_artifact` field is what points a
package's record at that file. Rule 6 of the guard proves the record and that digest list
agree on one hash, and the signature step proves the digest list was signed with the release
key; nothing here says anything about the key or whoever holds it.

Nothing was published to produce this log. The 6-of-6 line came from six synthetic binaries
in a temporary directory; the real `npm/` tree in the repository has no archives, which is
why the in-tree gate prints `0 of 6`. `chaos-code-win32-x64` and `chaos-code-win32-arm64`
are still npm's `0.0.1-security` placeholder names and cannot be published as-is, which is a
separate open row.

The launcher's pre-launch check is a size comparison and a re-bootstrap, by design. It is
not a second digest verification of the installed binary, and a file of the recorded length
that is not the recorded file will run if it got into the chaos home by some path other than
these installers.
